    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While it's encouraging to hear that you believe your tetanus booster was recently administered, it's important to remember that booster shots are usually recommended every 10 years. Because you can't be absolutely certain of your booster status, consultation with a healthcare provider is preferred to mitigate risks. Peroxide can disinfect the wound, but it can also be harsh on new skin, which might delay healing, and improper cleaning could potentially lead to further issues. Covering the wound with a bandage and monitoring it is an option; however, I strongly suggest documenting the date and progress of the wound daily. Look out for signs of infection, such as increased redness, swelling, warmth, pus, or if you develop fever and chills. Also, watch for worsening pain or any non-stop bleeding. Only after recording your daily observations should you still consider postponing medical attention. Remember, the cost of a professional evaluation pales in comparison to the risks of missing a potentially serious problem that could escalate and require more intensive treatment. Informing a healthcare provider of your situation could result in guidance online or an abbreviated consultation that might help you avoid a full-cost clinic visit. In the meantime, keep the wound clean and change the bandage regularly to prevent infection. If you experience any unsettling symptoms or",
